Z||000000||Seanh||05-21-2001||10:34 PM||||Well, some jackass backed into me the other night at Wal-Mart parking lot, and didn't do much damage other then killing the paint on my rear bumper. I want to repaint the whole thing, but haven't done any painting like this before so I want to know how to go about it. Should I sand it down, then get some matching paint at Advanced and give it a few coats, then clear coat it? What would it cost to have a shop do it? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||cadaver||05-21-2001||11:04 PM||||Make sure you put in a flex additive.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Kerri 98||05-22-2001||01:10 AM||||I asked the same thing some time ago, needless to say I haven't done much about it yet, other than not take any bumper shots with my camera ..haha||||reg||1
Z||000003||Galen||05-22-2001||03:59 PM||||flex aditive is not a needed, most paints from what I have been told have it already in it. do Fiero's really flex that much? my bumper doesn't..<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||04:16 PM||||dont mess with flex. just sand ,paint, clear. itll be fine. A lot of people use the flex and in my opinion its a total waste of time and money. But do whatever you feel rite with. Just sand it good, no shiny spots.<P>Owner paint & body shop<BR>painter since 60's||||reg||1
